---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Q1 = `console.log("Score:", 7 + 3);`;
const Q2 = `let result = 2 + 6 / 2;
console.log(result);`;
const Q5 = `a ← 4
b ← 9
DISPLAY(b MOD a)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 1</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-yellow"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            Five exam-style questions covering everything from Activity 1.1
            through 1.8. There will be one of these at the end of every block,
            all year, and each is <b>cumulative</b> &mdash; later sets still
            contain questions about this material.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AP exam gives you <b class="text-sn-theme">70 questions in 120
            minutes</b>. That is <b class="text-sn-theme">1 minute 43 seconds
            each</b>, including the ones with code to read. Almost nobody is
            fast enough at first, and the only fix is starting early.
          </p>

          <h4>How to Do This One</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Set a timer for 9 minutes</b> before you start.
            </li>
            <li>
              When the timer ends, stop. Mark anything unfinished as unfinished
              &mdash; that is real information, not a failure.
            </li>
            <li>
              Work them out. Do not run the code. On exam day there is no
              computer.
            </li>
            <li>
              For each question, also write <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>. This
              is graded on completion, so an honest <i>unsure</i> costs you
              nothing and tells us both where to look.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Guessing beats leaving it blank.</b> There is no penalty for a
            wrong answer on the AP exam, so a blank is a guaranteed zero where a
            guess is not.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <p>
            Four of these have exactly one correct answer. One of them asks you
            to select <i>all</i> that apply &mdash; the real exam has eight
            questions like that, and they are the ones students lose points on
            by stopping at the first true statement.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>What does the following code display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Question 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <ol type="A" class="mt-3">
            <li><code>Score: 73</code></li>
            <li><code>Score: 10</code></li>
            <li><code>Score:10</code></li>
            <li><code>Score: 7 + 3</code></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>What value is displayed?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Question 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <ol type="A" class="mt-3">
            <li><code>4</code></li>
            <li><code>5</code></li>
            <li><code>8</code></li>
            <li><code>3.5</code></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3</h3>

          <p>
            A jar holds exactly 7 marbles. You have 30 marbles and fill as many
            jars as you can. Which expression gives the number of marbles left
            over?
          </p>

          <ol type="A" class="mt-3">
            <li><code>30 / 7</code></li>
            <li><code>30 % 7</code></li>
            <li><code>30 - 7</code></li>
            <li><code>7 % 30</code></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which of the following are true about the value that{" "}
            <code>prompt()</code> returns?
          </p>

          <ol type="A" class="mt-3">
            <li>It is text, even when the user types only digits.</li>
            <li>
              It can be used with <code>*</code> without casting and still
              produce a correct numeric result.
            </li>
            <li>
              It can be used with <code>+</code> without casting and still
              produce a correct numeric result.
            </li>
            <li>
              It is lost unless it is stored in a variable.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            The following is written in AP exam notation. What does it display?
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Q5}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Question 5"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <ol type="A" class="mt-3">
            <li><code>1</code></li>
            <li><code>2</code></li>
            <li><code>2.25</code></li>
            <li><code>4</code></li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6 &mdash; Data and Impact</h3>

          <p>A single byte can represent how many different values?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>8</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>16</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>256</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>1024</code></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Your answer to each question, with <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b></li>
            <li>Whether you finished inside 9 minutes, and if not, how far you got</li>
            <li>
              One sentence: which question took you longest, and what slowed you
              down?
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>This is graded on completion, not accuracy.</b> A thoughtful wrong
            answer earns the same as a right one. What is being built here is
            speed and stamina, and both require being allowed to get things
            wrong cheaply.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
